WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.972 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A playground has a corner cut out for a tree.

00:00:03.192 --> 00:00:06.506 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can we still work out how many paving tiles it needs?

00:00:07.156 --> 00:00:10.316 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An irregular outline does not stop us measuring.

00:00:10.536 --> 00:00:13.892 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us turn the tricky shape into familiar rectangles.

00:00:14.542 --> 00:00:18.791 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to solve practical problems with
irregular shapes.

00:00:19.441 --> 00:00:21.191 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:24.783 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can split a shape or subtract a missing rectangle.

00:00:25.003 --> 00:00:27.912 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can check every outside length for perimeter.

00:00:28.132 --> 00:00:31.526 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare designs using the required measure.

00:00:32.176 --> 00:00:37.526 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Build an L shape with square tiles, then slide a pencil
along possible dividing lines.

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:44.916 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can split its area into non-overlapping rectangles, or
start with a large rectangle and subtract the missing part.

00:00:45.136 --> 00:00:48.565 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both methods must cover exactly the same surface.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:53.748 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An L-shaped space fits inside an eight-metre by six-metre
rectangle.

00:00:53.968 --> 00:00:56.969 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A three-metre by two-metre corner is missing.

00:00:57.189 --> 00:01:01.980 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict whether its area is greater or smaller than
forty-eight square metres.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:06.082 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Subtract the missing six square metres from forty-eight.

00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:09.538 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The L-shaped area is forty-two square metres.

00:01:09.758 --> 00:01:14.766 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As a check, split it into an eight-by-four rectangle and a
five-by-two rectangle.

00:01:14.986 --> 00:01:18.170 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thirty-two plus ten also gives forty-two.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A larger design fits inside a ten-metre by eight-metre
rectangle, with a four-by-three corner missing.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.933 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plan the area calculation first.

00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:31.558 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the border measurement separate from the surface
measurement.

00:01:32.208 --> 00:01:35.538 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The usable area is sixty-eight square metres.

00:01:35.758 --> 00:01:40.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For its border, trace ten, five, four, three, six and eight
metres.

00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:43.211 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That perimeter is thirty-six metres.

00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:47.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different questions about the same design need different
measures.

00:01:47.693 --> 00:01:51.999 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Using the full eight-by-six rectangle would include the tree
corner.

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:54.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That overcounts six square metres.

00:01:54.993 --> 00:02:00.641 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Subtract the missing rectangle, or add two rectangles that
cover only the actual playground.

00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:05.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A six-by-five rectangle has a two-by-two corner removed.

00:02:05.757 --> 00:02:07.465 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What area remains?

00:02:11.465 --> 00:02:14.650 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thirty minus four leaves twenty-six square units.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:21.869 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The missing corner is not part of the surface, so subtract
it once, rather than including the full bounding rectangle.

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:24.800 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the measure the problem needs.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.509 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For area, split or subtract rectangles; for perimeter, trace
every outside edge.

00:02:30.729 --> 00:02:35.353 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw a garden on squared paper and explain a paving plan and
a fencing plan.

00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:38.895 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check that your answers describe the actual design.

00:02:39.115 --> 00:02:41.946 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:42.166 --> 00:02:46.598 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
